  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 225 PM MDT Tue Aug 18 2026\n\nEnsemble guidance continues to be in support of ridging at 500-mb \noverhead through the duration of the long term period. At the same \ntime, a 500-mb shortwave trough looks to be traveling \nanticyclonically around the ridge, with a high amplitude 850-mb \ntrough at its leading edge. There are indications that a surface low \nwill be able to form across the High Plains in association with this \n850-mb feature, and move over or just south of the forecast region \nSaturday and Sunday. \n\n\n***Saturday/Sunday***\n\nSoutherly flow underneath the ridge alongside solar heating looks to \nfavor hot conditions Saturday and Sunday, with highs forecast in the \nmid to upper 90s, and possibly low 100s in some spots. In addition, \nGEFS 850-mb height guidance is in support of a high across portions \nof Eastern Texas during this period, which would prevent moisture \nfrom entering the southerly return flow. This is expected to promote \ndry conditions across portions of East-Central Colorado and West-\nCentral Kansas, where relative humidities (RH) may drop into the \nlower 20s to upper teens. While RH values are forecast to be a bit \nlow, fire weather conditions are not yet expected either Saturday or \nSunday, as wind gusts are not favored to exceed 25 mph except in Kit \nCarson and Yuma Counties in Colorado, where wind gusts may reach the \n25-30 mph range. Still, LREF guidance shows that RH values have less \nthan a 20% chance of meeting criteria for critical fire weather \nconditions (less than 15% RH) across nearly the entire forecast \narea. Confidence in a Red Flag Warning being needed is less than 5% \nboth Saturday and Sunday. \n\nForecast guidance continues to suggest that showers and \nthunderstorms are also possible during this period in association \nwith convergence from the surface low pressure. Even so, NBM 24 hr \nprecipitation guidance shows a 1 in 3 chance or less across the \nforecast area both days for greater than 0.1 inches of precipitation \nto be experienced. Any storms that develop are not expected to be \nsevere, as LREF guidance shows a 2 in 3 chance or better that sfc-\n500mb wind shear will be below 30 kts. Currently, the greatest \nconcern with these showers is that they may be able to produce dry \nlightning, particularly across East-Central Colorado where RH values \nare lowest, and NBM guidance shows the highest chances of showers \noccurring. \n\n\n***Monday/Tuesday***\n\nAs the surface low moves well south of the forecast area, a weak \ncold front may lower temperatures a couple of degrees into the low \nto mid 90s. Chances for precipitation increase a little bit during \nthis period, especially on Monday when NBM 24 hr precipitation \nguidance shows a 40-60% chance of receiving greater than 0.1 inches \nof rain across portions of Eastern Colorado. This would be in \nassociation with a cold front. The risk for severe storms continues \nto be low, as LREF guidance shows similar probabilities for wind \nshear as Saturday and Sunday. RH values look to improve as well, as \ntemperatures drop a little, and easterly winds across portions of \nCentral and East Kansas feed slightly higher dew points into the \nforecast area. This would reduce risks for critical fire\nweather.",
